<section class="net-reading net-syllabus-paper" id="net-syllabus-paper-2" aria-labelledby="net-syllabus-english">
  <p class="eyebrow">Paper 2 · Subject code 30</p>
  <h2 id="net-syllabus-english">English Literature: the ten units</h2>
  <p>Open a unit for a study-guide overview. The first four units cover literature across English-language traditions and also involve comprehension, critical reading and thinking. Use the <a href="https://www.ugcnetonline.in/syllabus-new.php" rel="noopener">official UGC syllabus</a> as your final reference.</p>

  <details class="net-syllabus-unit" open>
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">I</span><span class="net-unit-title">Drama</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Greek and Roman drama; English drama from miracle and morality plays through Marlowe, Shakespeare, Jonson and Webster; Restoration comedy; Ibsen and Shaw; modern and post-war drama, including Beckett, Pinter and Osborne; and Indian English playwrights such as Karnad and Tendulkar.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">II</span><span class="net-unit-title">Poetry</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Chaucer and medieval poetry, the sonnet tradition, Spenser, the Metaphysicals, Milton, the Augustans, the Romantics, the Victorians, the Modernists and the Movement poets, alongside Indian, American and postcolonial poetry in English.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">III</span><span class="net-unit-title">Fiction, short story</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">The rise of the novel, nineteenth-century realism, Victorian and modernist fiction, stream of consciousness, postmodern and postcolonial novels, Indian English fiction, and the short story as a distinct form.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">IV</span><span class="net-unit-title">Non-Fictional Prose</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Essays, biography, autobiography, travel writing, letters, journals and polemical prose, including Bacon, Addison and Steele, Lamb, Hazlitt, Ruskin, Arnold, Orwell and Woolf.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">V</span><span class="net-unit-title">Language: Basic concepts, theories and pedagogy. English in Use.</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Phonetics and phonology, morphology, syntax, semantics, sociolinguistics, varieties of English, error analysis, English language teaching and practical English usage.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">VI</span><span class="net-unit-title">English in India: history, evolution and futures</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Colonial education and Macaulay’s Minute, the growth of English studies in India, Indian English, translation, the relationship between English and Indian languages, and debates about English in education.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">VII</span><span class="net-unit-title">Cultural Studies</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">The Birmingham school, ideology and hegemony, popular culture, media, subculture, gender, identity, diaspora and globalisation, including Williams, Hoggart, Hall, Gramsci and Bourdieu.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">VIII</span><span class="net-unit-title">Literary Criticism</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Classical, Renaissance and Neoclassical criticism; Dryden, Johnson, Wordsworth and Coleridge; Arnold and Eliot; I. A. Richards, Leavis and the New Critics.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">IX</span><span class="net-unit-title">Literary Theory post World War II</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Structuralism, semiotics, poststructuralism, deconstruction, psychoanalysis, Marxism, feminism, reader-response theory, New Historicism, postcolonialism, queer theory and ecocriticism.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="net-syllabus-unit">
    <summary><span class="net-unit-number">X</span><span class="net-unit-title">Research Methods and Materials in English</span><img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="22" height="22"></summary>
    <p class="net-unit-description">Research design, the thesis and dissertation, citation and documentation, MLA style, plagiarism, academic ethics, bibliography, reference tools, archives and digital resources.</p>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="net-reading net-syllabus-paper" id="net-syllabus-paper-1" aria-labelledby="net-syllabus-general">
  <p class="eyebrow">Paper 1 · Common paper</p>
  <h2 id="net-syllabus-general">General Paper on Teaching &amp; Research Aptitude</h2>
  <p>Paper 1 is common to all subjects. Its units cover teaching and research capabilities, reasoning, comprehension, communication, data, technology, the environment and higher education.</p>
  <ol class="net-paper-one-units">
    <li>Teaching Aptitude</li>
    <li>Research Aptitude</li>
    <li>Comprehension</li>
    <li>Communication</li>
    <li>Mathematical Reasoning and Aptitude</li>
    <li>Logical Reasoning</li>
    <li>Data Interpretation</li>
    <li>Information and Communication Technology (ICT)</li>
    <li>People, Development and Environment</li>
    <li>Higher Education System</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="net-source-note">Source: <a href="https://www.ugcnetonline.in/syllabus-new.php" rel="noopener">UGC’s official syllabus index</a>, including Paper 1 (code 00) and English (code 30). The English unit summaries above are study-guide notes, rather than additional official unit titles.</p>
